        <h2 id="fair-allocation">
          5. Fair Resource Allocation: Leave Slots, Training &amp; Perks
        </h2>

        <p>
          One of the most practically useful HR applications is resource
          allocation. Whenever demand for something limited outstrips supply,
          someone has to decide who gets it — and that decision, however
          well-meant, creates winners, losers, and doubts about fairness.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>HR Resource Situation</th>
                <th>Traditional Method</th>
                <th>Spin Wheel Approach</th>
                <th>Key Benefit</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Multiple staff request the same holiday slot</td>
                <td>Manager discretion or seniority</td>
                <td>All requestors added, spin live</td>
                <td class="check">✔ No seniority bias</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Oversubscribed training course</td>
                <td>First-come-first-served or manager sign-off</td>
                <td>All applicants added, spin per seat</td>
                <td class="check">✔ Transparent allocation</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Limited premium desks / hot desks</td>
                <td>Seniority or manager assignment</td>
                <td>Weekly or monthly spin for premium spots</td>
                <td class="check">✔ Everyone gets a turn</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Conference ticket allocation</td>
                <td>Manager picks based on role fit</td>
                <td>Qualifying names on wheel, live draw</td>
                <td class="check">✔ Verified fair process</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Perk selection (WFH days, flexible Fridays)</td>
                <td>Ad hoc or informal negotiation</td>
                <td>Monthly draw from eligible pool</td>
                <td class="check">✔ Predictable rotation</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The wheel doesn't remove HR's judgment entirely — deciding who
          qualifies for the draw is still a human call. But once the eligible
          pool is set, allocation runs through a process everyone can watch and
          trust. Our
          <a
            href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/how-to-run-online-giveaway"
            >step-by-step guide to fair prize draws</a
          >
          covers the mechanics in full.
        </p>

        <div class="callout yellow">
          <div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div>
          <strong>Important consideration</strong>
          Always communicate eligibility criteria before running a wheel draw.
          The fairness of the wheel depends entirely on the fairness of who's
          added to it — a transparent, pre-agreed entry list is what makes the
          result universally accepted.
        </div>

        <h2 id="onboarding">6. Onboarding &amp; New Hire Integration</h2>

        <p>
          The first weeks at a new job are full of quiet social friction — new
          hires aren't sure where they fit, existing staff aren't sure how to
          bring them in, and HR is trying to speed up a process that usually
          takes months. A spin wheel turns out to be a surprisingly effective
          way to lower that barrier fast.
        </p>

        <div class="use-case">
          <div class="use-case-header">
            <div class="uc-icon">☕</div>
            <h3>Onboarding Buddy Assignment</h3>
            <div class="uc-tag">Day 1 · Integration</div>
          </div>
          <div class="use-case-body">
            <p>
