        <h2 id="setup-guide">7. Setting Up Your HR Wheel in 5 Minutes</h2>

        <p>
          A common reason spin wheels haven't spread further inside HR teams is
          the mistaken idea that they need technical setup. They don't. Here's
          the exact process for building a reusable recognition wheel from
          scratch.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Open spinthewheelsonline.com</strong> in any browser — no
            account, no download, no login. Works on Chrome, Firefox, Safari,
            and Edge, on laptop, tablet, or phone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Clear the default entries</strong> and paste in your team
            member names. For a 20-person team this takes about 90 seconds, and
            copy-pasting from a spreadsheet or Slack member list works cleanly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Adjust the wheel colours</strong> if you like — optional,
            but noticeable when it's projected during an all-hands.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Enable "Remove after spin"</strong> for anything that needs
            to work through a full list without repeats — speaking order, prize
            tiers, buddy assignments.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Bookmark the wheel's URL</strong> — entries are preserved in
            the link itself. Save separate bookmarks for recognition,
            icebreakers, and activity selection; no login required, the link is
            the save.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Screen-share during the event</strong> — the spin animation
            and sound both carry through on Zoom, Microsoft Teams, and Google
            Meet screen-shares.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="callout purple">
          <div class="callout-icon">🔖</div>
          <strong>Pro tip: build an HR wheel library</strong>
          Spend 20 minutes before your next team event building three wheels:
          (1) a recognition wheel with the full team, (2) an icebreaker wheel
          with 25 questions, (3) an activity selector for the event. Bookmark
          all three. From then on, every event has a ready engagement toolkit
          that needs zero extra prep.
        </div>

        <h2 id="best-practices">8. Best Practices for HR-Led Spin Events</h2>

        <p>
          These are the habits that separate HR teams getting lasting results
          from those who try it once and drop it.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Announce the process before the result.</strong> Explain
            who's in the draw and why, before spinning. That thirty-second
            explanation is what gives the result its legitimacy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Run it live, not offline.</strong> Spinning privately and
            announcing the result afterward loses most of the benefit — the
            transparency is the entire point.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>State the "wheel is final" norm from the first use.</strong>
            Before the first spin, say clearly: "We're going with whatever the
            wheel picks — no re-spins." This is what keeps the tool from being
            ignored the first time a result feels inconvenient.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Use it for more than one thing.</strong> Icebreakers,
            speaking order, team pairings, and activity selection all count. The
            more it's woven into normal operations, the more weight each
            individual draw carries.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Be transparent about weighting.</strong> "Entries are
            proportional to peer nominations received" builds trust rather than
            suspicion around the process.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep a record for equity auditing.</strong> Screenshot key
            draws, especially for resource allocation. Over a year this lets you
            show the distribution of outcomes has genuinely been fair.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Match the tool to the task.</strong> A spin wheel suits name
            draws and activity selection; a
            <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/yes-no-wheel"
              >Yes or No Wheel</a
            >
            suits binary calls. See our
            <a
              href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/spin-wheel-vs-other-random-pickers"
              >comparison of random pickers</a
            >
            for more.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="pitfalls">9. Pitfalls to Avoid</h2>
        <p>
          A handful of avoidable mistakes account for most HR spin-wheel
          programmes that quietly fizzle out.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Letting eligibility stay vague.</strong> If people aren't
            sure who was actually in the draw, the "fairness" benefit disappears
            even if the spin itself was genuinely random.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Overriding a result you don't like.</strong> One overridden
            spin undoes months of built-up trust in the process. If a result
            truly can't stand, exclude that entry next time instead of ignoring
            it this time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Using it only for good news.</strong> A wheel that only ever
            appears for prizes and praise can feel like a gimmick. Folding it
            into ordinary meeting rhythm — icebreakers, speaking order — makes
            it feel like infrastructure, not decoration.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Skipping the explanation.</strong> A spin that appears with
            no context reads as arbitrary. Thirty seconds of setup before each
            draw is what turns randomness into fairness in people's minds.
          </li>
        </ul>

